  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nUpper-level ridge over the Four Corners region will drift a bit\neastward into the Plains this weekend and early next week \nresulting in height rises. The result will be a return to very\nhot temperatures, with high temperatures reaching back above\n100. Temperatures across the forecast area today are expected to\nbe generally on the order of 95-100 with 100-103 across western\nand southern counties. Widespread 100-105 are expected Saturday\nwith 102-107 by Sunday. After collaboration with neighboring\noffices, the consensus was uncertainty from potential overnight\nand morning cloud cover will likely preclude the need for any \nheat headlines today, but may be needed in upcoming days.\n\nWith a relative break in storm chances through early next week,\nPOPs come back strong Wednesday through Friday. NBM POPs in this\ntime frame increase to 40-70%. With the expected breakdown of\nthe upper ridge, coupled with a frontal passage, seems\nreasonable chances increase. Uncertainty remains on placement of\nthe front and timing, but NBM probabilities and GEFS/ENS/GEPS\nensemble do suggest at a potential for heavier rains. Timing and\nlocations will need to be ironed out as we get closer.",
